# Global default change → offer apply to existing

Some settings are **global defaults** that new entities inherit (new subscriptions, new notification
rows). Changing that default must not silently rewrite every existing entity — and must not leave
the user without a way to do that rewrite when they want it.

## When this applies

A toggle (or similar control) is in this category when **both** are true:

1. It sets a **default for future** entities (subscribe, enable notifications, enable auto download).
2. Existing entities already hold their **own** copy of that value (per-channel notification row,
   per-channel auto-download row, per-channel cellular override).

Examples today:

| Surface | Setting |
| ------- | ------- |
| Web + mobile Notifications | Auto-enable notifications when I subscribe |
| Web + mobile Notifications | Default notification types (`new-item`, …) |
| Mobile Downloads | Auto download new subscriptions |
| Mobile Downloads | Download over cellular (global default) |

## Required UX

When the user changes such a default and the **affected existing count is greater than zero**, show a
confirm dialog **before** (or immediately after saving the default, with the bulk apply as the
optional second step — either order is fine as long as the choice is explicit):

- **Apply to all** — update every matching existing entity to the new value.
- **Only new** (cancel / secondary) — keep existing entities as they are; only future entities
  inherit the new default.

Skip the dialog when the affected count is **0** (nothing to apply).

### Components

| Surface | Dialog |
| ------- | ------ |
| Mobile | `ConfirmDialog` (`apps/mobile/src/components/feedback/ConfirmDialog.tsx`) |
| Web | `Modal` + `Modal.Actions` |

Copy is sentence case and goes through i18n. Prefer a shared shape: title naming the setting, body
with `{count}`, confirm = apply to all, cancel = only new / keep existing.

## Don't

- Don't silently bulk-update every podcast when the global flips.
- Don't omit the dialog because "most users have few subscriptions".
- Don't invent a toast or snackbar for this choice — it needs an explicit decision.
- Don't apply this pattern to prefs that are **not** inherited per entity (theme, language, quota
  size, Wi‑Fi-only as a single device-wide gate with no per-channel copy).

